A||N||2||DaveL||Clunking Ferraro||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||11-24-2000||08:26 AM||||I had been getting a nasty clunk when putting my 87 SE 2.5 auto into gear. My mechanic replaced the engine mount, which was broken, and the dogbone. Now the major clunk is gone, but I still get an occasional less serious clunk when putting it in reverse. Definitely from the right rear. Any suggestions? I get an occasional clunk from the front end that sounds like control arm bushings, but not that bad.||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||11-24-2000||09:43 AM||||Ferraro? ok...<P>read the article about the torque struts in the engine area of my cave. It applies to you. <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>the fiero cave.</A><P>at the very lest you need to dig around and see what's hitting and see if you can adjust it to not hit anymore. check over the exhaust and the air cleaner very carfully. look for signs of the air cleaner hitting the trunk wall.<P>if you aren't sure about the air cleaner.... smear some grease on the edge of the lid where it faces the trunk. see if the grease gets transfered to the trunk wall. If it does then you have a problem that if you don't fix it will wreck the TBI unit and/or intake manifold.||||reg||4
Z||000002||deceler8||11-24-2000||10:32 AM||||Why is it that the phrase "clunking Ferraro" sounds dirty ?<P>Sounds like something overheard in a New York City bar...||||reg||1
